A||N||10||Will||Fire in the hole! LT1/HEI hybrid distributor works||||1||
Z||000000||Will||02-11-2001||11:51 AM||||Yesterday my father and I fired up a TPI 400 SBC using an LT1 ECM. The was accomplished by using an HEI distributor with an LT1's opti-spark crankshaft position sensor.<BR>We will use the second prototype to run a 500 cid Cadillac engine, and the third with an as yet undetermined engine, probably a big block. Once that is done we should have enough streamlining of the manufacturing process to start marketing these distributors.<P>The upshot of this is that ANY distributor based GM V8 can be run by the most advanced distributor engine management ever designed.<P>Software to reprogram LT1 computers can be had for as little as $250 from <A HREF="http://www.carputing.com," TARGET=_blank>www.carputing.com,</A>  and the computers themselves can be had from junkyards for less than $100. This makes the LT1 solution much less expensive than programmable aftermarket ECM's or custom programs, while at the same time being more advanced than aftermarket systems.<P>Anyone thinking of building a fuel injected SBC Fiero now has another option.||||reg||1
Z||000001||gabriel||02-11-2001||01:08 PM||||Will, your link doesn't work.  Anyone wanting to go there can just enter  <A HREF="http://www.carputing.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.carputing.com/</A> <P>(your link contains the comma  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )||||reg||1
Z||000002||gabriel||02-11-2001||01:16 PM||||Oh, and by the way Will:<P>  Tell me how to do that for my 97 3800SC/4T65E, and youve got my vote!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>What do you think Terryk?||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||02-11-2001||02:28 PM||||No, I would dump the 4T65E and get a 4T60E and use a 92/93 or 94/95 ECM. Otherwise use the PCM.<P>I'm not sure what the motivation was to run a TPI on an LT1 ECM. The 7165 is so hacked I wouldn't consider an LT1.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||gabriel||02-11-2001||06:08 PM||||Terryk:<P>  Did you read the page about converting the OBD-II PCM to an OBD-I?<P>Hmmmm ...........<P>It's not for the 3800, but I was wondering if that might be possible for my PCM ...<P>I'm new to the ECM/PCM thing (just wrenched a lot) but would that be ... practical? <P>  Is there a performance hack for my combo?<P>Any of the above might be easier than changing trannies at this point.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||02-11-2001||06:16 PM||||We like the LT1 ECM because:<P>1) '94+ are sequential rather than batch fire<BR>2) '94+ also use both MAF and MAP sensors<BR>3) Y and B Body versions use two knock sensors<BR>4) Lower material investment: only a cable needed to program from a computer; no chip reading/burning hardware required.||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||02-11-2001||06:25 PM||||I understand the differences. Like all SFI, they likely switch to batch above 3000 RPM. <P>I think the investment in flash vs eproms is a wash. <P>Hey, you got it working, so charge on!<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 02-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Will||02-12-2001||10:04 PM||||Isn't sequential worth several horsepower and a few mpg on 3800's, as compared to batch fire?<BR>Below 3K is where I care about gas mileage and driveability. I think it's worth it, cost being equal.<P>For those of us who are really crazy, the combination of MAF and MAP makes the system more forced induction friendly.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 02-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||gabriel||02-12-2001||10:24 PM||||&gt;Isn't sequential worth several horsepower and a few mpg on 3800's, as compared to batch fire?<P> Yeah, I guess, but I believe the LT1 is a V-8 system (correct me if I'm wrong), so how does it apply to the 3800SC?<P>I really have no information for any hacks for my system or the OBD-1, so I'll just keep looking.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfixit58||02-13-2001||07:48 AM||||Will,<P>That sounds great. I've been toying with the idea of fuel injection for my 69 Firebird 400. The project is quite a ways off for now. So, you will have plenty of time to "tweek" your new distributor.<P>A couple of questions: What kind of fuel injection system are you planning on using on the 500 Caddy? Are you drilling and tapping the stock intake for the injectors? What kind of throttle body are you using?<P>Keep me posted,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>By the way, I've sold my carbed 350 and have started pulling the wiring harness from the 89 GTA. I hope to pull the 350 TPI and 700r4 and start the detail work in the next few weeks.||||reg||14
Z||000010||Will||02-13-2001||10:28 AM||||There was a very rare fuel injection option for the 550 Caddys. We happened to find one of those manifolds. It uses low impedance injectors, though, so we'll have to build an interface to let the LT1 high-Z drivers work the peak&hold injectors. Don't know what kind of throttle body right off. I think my dad has one in mind. I'll ask him later.<P>Imagine one of those in a Fiero! They are about 80 lbs lighter than a 454. Only 50 lbs heavier than an SBC. It would be an MFin' tight fit, though.<P><BR>The manifold base for the TPI had to be modified a little to fit the 400 because of the differences in the intake bolts. If there's a machine shop in your area, it shouldn't be too hard. Street and Performance ( <A HREF="http://www.tunedport.com" TARGET=_blank>www.tunedport.com</A>  ) may even have the right thing for you in stock.||||reg||1
